    "q-320017": {
      "dom_id": "step-q320017",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $ABC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |",
      "worked_solution": "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix. Therefore,\n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$ gives a $3\\times 3$ matrix, which would require finding $9$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$ gives a $2\\times 1$ matrix, which requires finding $2$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -15 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
